The `$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x` gets a "viewing" of the value from the `Old_arrListLastIndexOfX` variable. The `$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x` gets a "viewing" of the value from the `New_arrListLastIndexOfX` variable.

The `Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);` is used to verify this condition. If the condition fails to be true, the Assert.Fail method is called, which may stop debugging if there is a breakpoint or the code is running under the debugger.--------------------The "PUT_SetContract" method in your code will set the value of `index` in `arrList` array to `x`. The comments are written using the @@@ keyword to indicate the conditions that should be true after executing this method. One of the conditions to be true is that the last position of `x` in the old array is less than or equal to the same in the new array, which implies that the index of `x` in the new `arrList` after the operation should be the same or greater than the index of `x` in the old array.

The C# code with Debug.Assert conditions corresponding to the comments is as follows:

```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_SetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            arrList[index] = x;

            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            // check the condition "last position of x in old array is <= same in new array"
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

            // add these two lines to verify each value
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX);
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

            // add these three lines to verify each boolean value
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}

```
In the code snippets, `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue` is used to make certain assumptions about the parameters (arrList, x, and index) before the test. `PexObserve.ValueForViewing` is used to keep track of values of variables for verification. The `Debug.Assert` checks conditions that should be true in certain situations to verify that method works as expected according to the contracts. 

This code is using `Debug.Assert` because it is designed for non-production environments where assumptions about the system should be true. It will halt the execution if they are not. Be careful using this method in production environments as it may lead your application to behave unexpectedly.--------------------Here is the code for the debug assert statements corresponding to the comments:

```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListIndexOfX, "The last position of x in old array is not <= same in new array.");
```--------------------```cs
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_SetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            arrList[index] = x;

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}

```

The code above reads the given C# code, looks for the comments that start with "@@@", and replaces them with Debug.Assert statements according to the rule specified by the "@@@" comment. The Debug.Assert statement in C# is used for runtime assertions. It is a means to perform runtime checks. The expression under the Assert statement is evaluated only if the application is built in debug mode. If the expression results in a false value, the developer is notified that the assert condition has failed.--------------------